#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""The privileged half of UI Apply: switch the AES67 service between accepted
profiles on request, verify the result, roll back on failure, leave a receipt.

The web service (usb16x16-web) runs unprivileged with NoNewPrivileges and can
write exactly one kind of thing outside its preset store: files in the inbox.
A root systemd path unit (usb16x16-apply.path) runs this program whenever the
inbox is not empty. Nothing in a request is executed; it names an accepted
profile and the profile the browser believed was live, and this program
decides everything else from the live configuration.

Two directories (1.9.0), neither owned by the web user:

  inbox   /var/spool/usb-aoip/inbox (--inbox, USB16X16_APPLY_INBOX), owned
          root:<web group>, mode 1730: the web user can create files in it
          but cannot list it or remove or replace another's. For each request
          it picks a fresh random id (8-32 lower-case hex) and writes any
          uploaded artifacts first, <id>.dsp and <id>-<rate>.dsp, then the
          request as <id>.request.tmp renamed to <id>.request. The JSON body
          carries the same id. This program processes every <id>.request,
          oldest mtime first, and scans again before it exits. Every read from
          the inbox opens the name with O_NOFOLLOW | O_NONBLOCK, requires a
          regular file with one link, reads at most a cap (64 KiB for a
          request, 256 KiB for an artifact) and removes the name. A request
          whose body id differs from its file name is refused. Other files
          older than 10 minutes are removed. Nothing is written to the inbox.
  state   /var/lib/usb16x16-apply (--state, USB16X16_APPLY_STATE), root:root
          0755, created if missing; this program refuses to run if it is a
          symlink, not a directory, not owned by the user running it, or
          writable by group or others. It holds current.json (the operation
          in progress), receipts/<id>.json (one per request; an existing
          receipt makes a request a replay), apply.lock (one operation at a
          time), rate-history.json (the arbiter's last change), check/
          (0700, the loader check's temporary copy) and, since 1.10.0,
          bundles/ (0750, group the inbox's: support bundles the web service
          serves) and, since 1.11.0, monitor-limits (0644: the monitor's
          limits, which the Dante owner and the web service read). Every
          write here goes
          through a temporary file created with O_EXCL | O_NOFOLLOW, then
          fsync and rename.

Every request's envelope (id, requestedAt within 120 s, no receipt yet) is
checked before its kind acts. The kind "rate" is taken only from the
--rate-request trigger, never from the inbox.

One operation, in order, each step recorded in current.json as it happens:

  validating   the request is fresh, unseen, well formed; the profile exists
               in /etc/usb16x16/aes67.conf with STATUS=accepted; the browser's
               expected profile is the live one (a stale page is refused)
  stopping     systemctl stop usb16x16-aes67.service
  switching    usb16x16-aes67-supervisor switch-profile NAME (the supervisor
               validates the block and the preset hash; the service is down,
               so it does not restart anything itself)
  starting     systemctl start usb16x16-aes67.service
  verifying    the supervisor reaches STAGE running, the bridge's telemetry
               line is fresh with zero direction restarts, no RECOVER, and
               the bridge log carries the DSP loaded / engaged lines the
               profile calls for (or none, for a profile without a preset);
               then a settle window with the same checks
  done         or: rolling-back to the previous profile through the same
               steps, then rolled-back, or failed if that also fails

A profile switch restarts audio and draws a new latency level; that is the
operation the user asked for, stated in the UI before the request is sent.
The applier never edits a profile block, never installs a preset, and never
touches the USB gadget, the daemon or the module: switch-profile is the only
write, and it is the supervisor's own command.

A second request kind, "preset" (2026-09-11), installs a user preset the
browser compiled with work/dsp-compiler and switches to it. The artifact text
arrives beside the request in the inbox as <id>.dsp; this program
recomputes its hash, parses it with the same bounds as the bridge's loader,
requires every biquad section to be stable and the total to stay within the
count the loaded soak passed, loads it through the bridge's own code
(usb16x16-dsp-offline --bench 1), installs it root-owned under
/etc/usb16x16/dsp/user-<slug>.dsp, writes a profile block whose transport
fields are dsp-direct's copied verbatim plus the preset path and hash, has the
supervisor's own parser validate the resulting configuration before it
replaces the live one, and only then runs the switch above. Such a block is
STATUS=experimental (the supervisor's own vocabulary; it switches only to
accepted or experimental profiles) and is identified as a user preset by its
user-<slug> name; it is never accepted: the guards stand in for a soak, and
the UI says so. A third kind, "remove", deletes a user block and its file when it is not
the live profile. Eight user presets at most, one per slug.

Two more kinds (2026-09-13, work/phase4-bridge/LIVE_COEFFICIENTS_DESIGN.md
step 4) update a running user preset without a restart. "live" takes an
artifact the same way "preset" does, runs the same guards, and additionally
requires the live profile to be a user preset whose block carries DSP_LIVE=1
and whose installed file has the same *shape* (every count and index in the
artifact, with gains, delays and coefficients blanked: artifact_shape()
below, the same text bridge-artifact.mjs and DspStage::shape() produce, hashed
with SHA-256). It then writes the artifact to the bridge's live file under
/run/usb16x16 by rename and waits for the bridge's status line to report
that hash applied. Nothing is installed: a restart comes back on the
installed file, by the bridge's own rule (a live file older than the process
is ignored). "commit" copies the applied live file over the installed
user-<slug>.dsp and rewrites the block's DSP_SHA256, through the same
validate-on-a-copy and atomic replace as "preset", with no restart: the
running coefficients already equal the file. Neither kind ever touches an
accepted profile.

A sixth kind, "shutdown" (2026-09-14), powers the Pi off from the page. It
takes the envelope checks only (fresh, unseen, well formed), stops
usb16x16-aes67.service so the bridge and daemon tear down in order before
the rest of the system does, writes its receipt, and then asks systemd for
`systemctl poweroff --no-block`. The receipt is written first on purpose:
it is the last thing the browser can read before the connection drops. The
USB gadget service is not touched; systemd stops it with everything else.
The operator then unplugs the USB data cable, then PoE, as the handoff's
clean shutdown says.

Five more kinds (2026-11-17, 1.10.0; the page's System actions,
docs/web/REDESIGN-2026-11.md phase 2) take the envelope checks only, as
"shutdown" does, and nothing else from the request but a password's user and
hash. "reboot" is "shutdown" with `systemctl reboot --no-block` (step
rebooting), its receipt written first for the same reason. "restart-audio"
stops and starts the audio unit on the configuration as it is: no file is
written (on the Dante stack owner.conf must first pass the wrapper's parser,
so a configuration that cannot start is refused with audio untouched), then
the switch's verify and settle; a bad start is failed, there being nothing to
roll back to. "restart-clock" (the Dante stack only) restarts
usb16x16-statime.service, waits for it, waits for the endpoint, which the
installed unit's PartOf= restarts with the clock, and verifies it as
restart-audio does; whether the endpoint got a new start is read afterwards,
not assumed. "bundle" writes one JSON document for support under the state
directory as bundles/<id>.json (0750 / 0640, group the inbox's, the newest
two kept): versions, hashes of the installed programs, the running profile
and rate, the start check's line, unit states, the stack's configuration,
current.json and the last ten receipts, and 400 journal lines a unit, capped
at 4 MiB by trimming journal lines oldest first. It never reads the login
file, anything under /etc/usb-aoip or key material (is_secret, checked before
every read). "password" replaces one existing user's line in the web
service's login file (USB16X16_WEB_AUTH, /etc/usb-aoip/web-auth) with a
pbkdf2_sha256 hash the web service made, read without following links and
written by exclusive temporary and rename with the file's owner, group and
mode kept and every other line kept byte for byte; a user with no line is
refused (a request never adds a login). The receipt names the user; the hash
appears in no receipt, step, error or output. Bundles and passwords restart
nothing, so a drain takes the next request after them in the same run.

Two more (2026-11-18, 1.11.0; the monitor block, REDESIGN-2026-11.md phase
3) write the monitor's limits, docs/web/MONITOR-FILES.md (b), to
monitor-limits in the state directory: the knob's maximum, the start rule
and level, and the reference lock ("monitor-limits", which is also how the
lock is undone), and one set's reference level and note ("monitor-reference",
refused while the lock is on). The file is read strictly; one that does not
parse is replaced by the defaults merged with the request and the receipt
says so (limitsReplaced). start_db and the references are clamped to max_db;
the receipt carries the limits as written. Nothing is restarted or
signalled (the owner polls the file), so a drain goes on after them. The
web service writes the monitor's own state file itself, without this
program: a knob must not cost a root process. Both stacks take the two
kinds (the code is the base class's); the web service offers them on the
Dante stack only, the one whose engine reads the file.

Exit status is 0 when a receipt was written (even for a refused or failed
request: the outcome is the receipt) or when nothing was pending, 2 for a
broken environment (including a state directory that fails its checks, or the
removed --spool option or USB16X16_APPLY_DIR variable).
"""

